  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nRest of Today to Monday.... Rain will continue to advance north\nthis afternoon into the evening thanks to persistent southerly \nflow along the axis of the trough aloft. Coverage of these \nshowers will decrease through the afternoon into the evening \nhours. Eventually expecting some more pop up isolated showers \nacross mainly southern Minnesota this evening into tonight if a\nLLJ can develop. There is just enough instability present for \nsome isolated lightning as well across Minnesota south of the \nTwin Cities metro. By daytime tomorrow this trough will be \nmostly to our east providing for a dry day. With cloud cover \nexpected to hold throughout the night temperatures will not be \nable to fall much as the clouds limit the radiational cooling. \nClouds should clear from west to east on Sunday which will allow\nfor some additional daytime heating across parts of western \nMinnesota where temperatures look likely to break the 70 degree \nmark. Upper 60s more likely to the east as cloud cover holds\nmuch of the day. Southerly flow and WAA as a high is off to our\neast on Monday will provide for fairly widespread highs \nbreaking into the lower 70s. \n\nMonday night and Tuesday... The next shortwave trough moves\nthrough the Northern Plain and Upper Midwest Monday night into\nTuesday. Rain will be expected along and in front of the\nsurface front associated with this wave aloft. Timing of this\nfrontal passage still varies between deterministic models and\ntheir ensemble membership. What looks likely with this is\nwidespread rain, mainly on Tuesday. What remains less certain is\nthe potential for heavy rainfall and the exact timing for the\nfrontal passage. Overall dynamics remain better to our north and\nsouth where there is greater agreement in ensemble members QPF.\nThere remain some solutions that would extend this higher QPF \ninto southern Minnesota and western Wisconsin, but there is much\nmore spread. A lot of this has to do with the timing of when\nfrontal passage occurs and what environment builds ahead of\nFROPA. As this event moves from the long term into the short \nterm period we should start to get a better idea on these finer \ndetails on the forecast. With rain forecast and therefore \nevaporative cooling temperatures look slightly cooler on Tuesday\ncompared to Monday, but continued WAA for much of the day will \nkeep temperatures on the slightly warmer side of normal in the \nupper 60s.\n\nRest of the week... Past Tuesday's system the ensembles start \nto scatter a lot more with respect to QPF. Across the global \ndeterministic models there is agreement in the jet stream being\nacross southern Canada to our north. This keeps us on the \nwarmer side and temperatures look to remain near to above normal\nfor much of the remainder of the week. The other side to this \nis more zonal flow at 500 mb which means rain chances will be \nmore dependent on smaller scale features rather than large \nsynoptic troughs. This is part of why we see such scattered QPF\ndata in the ensemble members for this period.",
